  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "Currently VRCJson.TryDeserializeFromJson and VRCJson.TrySerializeToJson have two big limitations :\n\n- DataDictionary keys need to be strings.\n\n- `Int`s are converted into `Double`.\n\nThose limitations forces anyone who wants to sync the DataDictitonary to work with strings as keys, and to use `double`s instead of `int`:\n\n- In the first case, keys need to be converted into strings, which could cause issues if someone need to call `GetKeys().Sort()` to get a sorted key/value pair list, keys need to be converted into numerical values first, which is a bit annoying.\n\n- In the second case, all numerical values needs to be converted into double, which can cause errors and bugs if the creator forgot to use `double` instead of `int`, just as an example would be storing player IDs (that are `int`s), so IDs need to be casted into `double` beforehand.\n\nMy suggestion would be to add an option to also serialize the data type of the DataToken, the outputed JSON could look like this \n\n```\n\n {\n\n \t\"Int:0\": \"String:value1\",\n\n \t\"Int:1\": \"String:value2\"\n\n }\n\n```\n\ninstead of \n\n```\n\n{\n\n \t\"0\": \"value1\",\n\n \t\"1\": \"value2\"\n\n }\n\n```\n\n this would make the string longer, but it would allow to serialize a wider range of data types. Maybe adding a new `JsonExportType` value like `JsonExportType.WithTypes` ?",
